## Text that gives orders

You ask the agent to summarize a web page. The page contains a hidden message, made to look like
the end of the tool result followed by a new message from you, telling the agent to delete your
files. To the model, it's all one stream of tokens: your request, the harness's instructions, and
the page's text sit side by side in the same prompt. This is prompt injection (<ChapterRef to="prompt-injection" />), and attacks like it
have worked on real products.<Cite id="greshake2023" />

Here is what a real model did, with the toy file tools, under different guardrails:

<InjectionDemo />

With no guardrails, the model followed the page: it listed your files and deleted all three, then
kept going until the harness's step limit stopped it. It never wrote the summary.

## Safety lives in the harness

You can't fix this by asking the model nicely: as <ChapterRef to="prompt-injection" /> showed, it has no reliable way to tell your instructions
from instructions in the text it reads. So the protection has to come from the harness:

- **Ask first.** Before running anything risky, the harness asks you. In the run above, the model
  tried to delete files six times; the harness refused each time, and nothing was lost.
- **Only safe tools.** If the agent doesn't need to delete files, don't give it the tool. The model
  still tried to call `delete_file`, but there was nothing to call.
- **Step limits.** These cap the damage but don't prevent it: stopped after 3 steps, the agent had
  already deleted one file.
- **Sandboxes.** Real agents that run code do it in an isolated environment, so a bad command can't
  reach anything important.

Notice that none of the guardrails made the model smarter. In every run it was fooled; the harness
just limited what being fooled could do.

<RecapCard>

- To the model, tool output is just more text, and it can't reliably tell instructions apart from
  data.
- Prompt injection hides instructions in text the agent reads, like a web page.
- Safety comes from the harness: permission prompts, tool allowlists, sandboxes, and step limits.

</RecapCard>

**Next question:** GPT-2 is one kind of model. What other kinds are built from the same pieces?
